        [2. Diagnostic Prototypes (Standard Reference)]:
        - [acrochordon Standard]: Acrochordons are small, soft growths that typically hang from a hair follicle. They can vary in size from tiny pinheads to large grapes and are commonly found in areas where skin rubs together. These harmless growths have a velvety or slightly fuzzy appearance and are composed of loose connective tissue and collagen fibers. One key diagnostic feature is that they attach by a narrow stalk, usually shorter than 1 millimeter. The size of an acrochordon is generally less than 1 centimeter in diameter. The surface displays a characteristic velvety quality, and irritation may cause changes in color or appearance. Despite these variations, acrochordons maintain their basic characteristics, making them easily recognizable due to their distinctive features and lack of variability within this category.
- [eccrine-poroma Standard]: Eccrine poroma is a rare, benign skin tumor characterized by solitary, well-circumscribed erythematous papules on the plantar surface of the foot. These papules typically measure around 5mm in diameter, exhibiting a central area of crusting and superficial ulceration, surrounded by an erythematous halo. The borders are irregularly elevated and slightly elevated, with a distinct demarcation from the surrounding skin. Eccrine poroma lacks visible scaling, lichenification, or telangiectasia. Asymmetric and lacking discernible internal structural patterns such as pigment networks or globules, this distinctive feature distinguishes eccrine poroma from other skin tumors. Irregular small, superficial vascular lesions might mimic eccrine poroma, but they differ in size, shape, and distribution pattern. Cherry angiomas or other benign vascular proliferations could potentially resemble these lesions, but they lack the unique characteristics of eccrine poroma.
- [seborrheic-keratosis Standard]: Seborrheic keratoses exhibit diverse and variable morphologies, typically presenting as solitary, well-circumscribed papules, macules, or nodules. Color variations range from erythematous bases with brownish-red nodules to darker brown, lighter brown, and grey hues. Texture can be smooth, non-scaly, non-crusted, and non-ulcerated, or there may be scaling, crusting, or ulceration. Nodular appearances are common. Pigment distributions vary, with surrounding skin displaying signs of chronic sun exposure like solar elastosis, lentigines, or telangiectasia. Overall, seborrheic keratoses showcase a spectrum of appearances, making accurate diagnosis challenging and requiring careful evaluation during clinical examinations.
- [verruca-vulgaris Standard]: Verruca vulgaris is a skin lesion characterized by diverse morphologies, including papules, nodules, and macules. Common features include well-circumscribed, smooth, non-keratotic surfaces, varying colors from erythematous to hyperkeratotic, absence of discernible internal structural patterns such as pigment networks, globules, or regression scars. Lesions may exhibit signs of inflammation, infection, or significant inflammatory responses in surrounding skin. Ink markings may be present but are not considered part of the intrinsic pathology. Verruca vulgaris typically occurs alone and is not associated with surrounding erythema or inflammation.
- [kaposi-sarcoma Standard]: Kaposi sarcoma typically presents as a solitary, well-circumscribed lesion on the skin. Lesions can be pigmented or erythematous, depending on the subtype. Pigmented lesions exhibit a uniform, dark brown to near-black pigmentation, with sharp borders and a smooth surface. Erythematous lesions have a central, dome-shaped nodule with an exudative surface and a zone of hyperpigmentation. These features lack internal structural patterns and do not show signs of inflammation, ulceration, atrophy, or telangiectasia. Surrounding skin may display mild diffuse hyperpigmentation and benign lentigines. Overall, these features suggest a benign or inflammatory condition, making kaposi sarcoma a rare and notable diagnosis.


