"summary": "Benign keratoses display various morphological characteristics, including discrete, elevated, solid papules with pigmentation ranging from brown to dark brown, sometimes displaying black hues. These lesions typically have well-defined borders and lack scaling, crusting, ulceration, atrophy, lichenification, or telangiectasia. They may present as single or multiple, isolated macules or papules, often concentrated on specific body regions like the dorsal thorax and upper back. Their distribution tends to be asymmetric, and they can manifest as either solitary or clustered lesions. While some cases might show internal structural patterns like pigment networks or regression areas, many do not. In general, benign keratoses maintain a stable and non-inflamed condition without signs of acute infection or active inflammation. However, it's essential to note that accurate diagnosis relies on comprehensive clinical examination and potentially histopathological evaluations."
- [mycosis-fungoides Standard]: Mycosis fungoides, also known as cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, presents with characteristic diagnostic features characterized by the development of hyperpigmented macules and patches on the skin. These lesions primarily manifest on the dorsal thoracic and upper lumbar regions, displaying a diverse array of brown to dark brown pigmentation. Lesions are typically solitary, well-circumscribed, and display sharp demarcation from surrounding skin. They may exhibit various textures, ranging from erythematous plaques to smooth, non-scaly surfaces. Asymmetry in lesion placement and form, as well as the absence of internal structural patterns such as pigment networks, globules, or regression scars, is common. While heterogeneity in pigmentation intensity may exist among various lesions, overall consistency within each individual lesion is observed.
- [nevus-lipomatosus-superficialis Standard]: Nevus-lipomatosus-superficialis is characterized by a solitary, well-circumscribed, dome-shaped skin lesion, ranging in size from 0.5cm to 1.5cm in diameter. The lesion displays a uniform, pale pink to flesh-colored hue, with no evidence of hyperpigmentation, hypopigmentation, or variegation. Its borders are sharply demarcated and regular, exhibiting a cohesive, non-infiltrative growth pattern. The surface is smooth, non-scaly, non-crusted, and non-ulcerated, devoid of telangiectasia or signs of atrophy. The lesion is symmetrically positioned on the skin and reveals no internal structural patterns such as pigment networks, globules, or regression areas. The surrounding skin appears normal with no erythema, edema, or associated inflammation. The overall morphology and clinical presentation align with a benign, solitary dermal nodule, likeliest being a sebaceous cyst or a similar harmless proliferative lesion. When pigmented, these lesions manifest as a macule featuring a central region of hypopigmentation or depigmentation, encompassed by an organized, slightly elevated, purplish-brown halo comprised of numerous tiny, discrete, dark brown to purplish macules. This arrangement forms a distinct, regular, circular border that distinctly separates the aberrant pigmentation from the encompassing normal skin, indicative of a well-defined margin. The surface remains smooth and non-scaly, devoid of crusting, ulceration, or telangiectasia. The overall structure maintains symmetry across the central depigmented zone, with the peripheral pigmented components arranging themselves into a concentric, ring-like pattern. No observable signs of lichenification, atrophy, or other textural anomalies exist. The coloration showcases heterogeneity, with the central area showcasing a lighter (hypopigmented) appearance while the peripheral ring displays a more uniform dark brown to purplish tone, signifying potential focal regression or central clearing within the lesion. Nevus-lipomatosus-superficialis generally manifests as a stable, non-inflamed skin anomaly.
- [metastatic-carcinoma Standard]: Metastatic carcinomas present with unique visual characteristics that distinguish them from other types of cancer. These include irregularly shaped erythematous skin lesions with active inflammation, central regression or necrosis leading to areas of central pallor or hypopigmentation, irregular borders, and lack of radial or bilateral symmetry. Active inflammatory processes can result in diffuse erythematous macular eruptions. Although certain cases may have faint markings for clinical tracking, these do not significantly impact the primary dermatological assessment. When evaluating metastatic carcinomas, focus on their distinctive morphological features rather than relying solely on specific imaging findings. Recent verified cases suggest that metastatic carcinomas often exhibit chronic, possibly malignant or inflammatory skin lesions with irregular borders, central ulcerations, and a rough, uneven surface texture. They may also demonstrate asymmetry and lack of radial or bilateral symmetry. In summary, metastatic carcinomas possess a characteristic set of visual features including irregular shapes, active inflammation, and a lack of symmetry, making them easily distinguishable from other types of cancer upon examination.
- [verruca-vulgaris Standard]: Verruca vulgaris is a skin lesion characterized by diverse morphologies, including papules, nodules, and macules. Common features include well-circumscribed, smooth, non-keratotic surfaces, varying colors from erythematous to hyperkeratotic, absence of discernible internal structural patterns such as pigment networks, globules, or regression scars. Lesions may exhibit signs of inflammation, infection, or significant inflammatory responses in surrounding skin. Ink markings may be present but are not considered part of the intrinsic pathology. Verruca vulgaris typically occurs alone and is not associated with surrounding erythema or inflammation.


